      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module assesses whether the paper's code could be run on its data, and — because `execute = TRUE` — **actually ran it**. We examined 2 R code files.",
          "#### Run order",
          "No ordering signal (no data dependency, `source()` call, or numeric filename prefix) distinguishes the run order of these files. They may need to run in a specific order that could not be determined automatically — check this before running.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Order = 1:2, File = c(\"undef_definer.R\", \"undef_user.R\"\n), \"Runs after\" = c(\"—\", \"undef_definer.R\"), Basis = c(\"none\", \n\"dependency\")), row.names = c(NA, -2L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "#### File paths",
          "To run against the Psych-DS layout, 0 referenced data paths would be rewritten to their new locations.",
          "#### Missing inputs",
          "Every data file the code reads is either produced by an earlier script or present in the repository.",
          "#### Execution",
          "**The code was run.** Each script ran in an isolated subprocess against a throwaway copy of the Psych-DS layout, in the run order above, with a 60-second per-script timeout. Of 2 scripts: 1 ran without error, 1 errored, 0 timed out, 0 were skipped (inputs unavailable), 0 could not run because one of its own dependencies is unavailable, and 0 did not parse. Running against current package versions: a break can reflect version drift, which argues for pinning versions. \n\n*a script hit an `object '...' not found` error, indicating it expected a variable another script defines. We inferred which script supplies it and re-ordered so that script runs first, and re-ran once. The outcomes above are from that corrected run.*",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(File = c(\"undef_definer.R\", \"undef_user.R\"), Outcome = c(\"ran_ok\", \n\"errored\"), Detail = c(\"\", \"undefined_variable\"), \"Time (s)\" = c(0.4, \n0.3)), row.names = c(NA, -2L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 15, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "**Undefined-variable errors**",
          "**1 script failed on an undefined variable or function** (`object '...' not found` / `could not find function \"...\"`). This typically means the script expects a variable or function that another script defines — i.e. it is meant to be sourced into a larger session, not run on its own — or the symbol is simply never created. When another of the paper's own script files defines the same name, this is called out below as a likely sourcing/copy-paste gap. Each is counted as an error (red).",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(File = \"undef_user.R\", \"Missing variable\" = \"helper_value\", \n    \"Defined in\" = \"undef_definer.R\"), row.names = c(NA, -1L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "*Expand a row below to see the output each script produced.*",
          "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Output — undef_definer.R (ran_ok)\" collapse=\"true\"}\n\n**Output (stdout):**\n````\n> helper_value <- 42\n> print(helper_value)\n[1] 42\n````\n\n:::\n",
          "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Output — undef_user.R (errored)\" collapse=\"true\"}\n\n**Output (stdout):**\n````\n> print(helper_value)\n````\n**Messages / errors (stderr):**\n````\nError in eval(e, envir = env) : object 'helper_value' not found\n````\n\n:::\n"
        ]
